ng the closeness of the September survey, or a late shift in the national environment, would move this toward Tossup territory.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Bill Cassidy (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Louisiana's Senate seat is open this year after Bill Cassidy lost renomination, and Republican Julia Letlow enters the general election as the clear favorite over Democrat Jamie Davis. The state's strong Republican tilt, Letlow's fundraising edge, and heavy outside spending on her behalf frame the race, and raters across the spectrum call it Solid or Safe R, with a few at Likely R. Prediction markets give Davis roughly a five to nine percent chance.\n\nStill, the race is not without noise. A September likely-voter poll sponsored by a Democratic firm showed Davis trailing by only four points, and the national environment appears favorable to Democrats, keeping the margin tighter than the state's usual blowouts. Davis has raised about two million dollars but remains badly outspent. Expect a Republican win, with the open-seat dynamic and thin polling leaving some room for a closer finish than recent Louisiana Senate races.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0491, "margin": -9.91}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0664, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0312, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0328, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0575, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0895, "margin": -8.0}]}